================================================================================
SESSION 2026-10-02 — WP-10 Increment N: FoundConnectionInserter prereqs (part 1)
================================================================================

Plan: port the Java TraceShover spring-over path, which the Phase-4.4
FoundConnectionInserter depends on, before touching the inserter itself.

1. Repaired the tree first — the build was broken by an earlier session:
   BasicBoard::insert_trace / check_polyline_trace / get_max_generated_id and
   the max_trace_half_width_ / min_trace_half_width_ bookkeeping had only
   declarations. Build + ctest green (655/655) after that.

2. Ported ShoveTraceAlgo::spring_over() and spring_over_obstacles() as the full
   Java TraceShover.java:611-875 ports, plus get_ignore_items_at_tie_pins().
   Java's "null / same object / new object" return is mapped to
   SpringOverResult{std::optional<Polyline>, bool changed}.

3. Prerequisites ported in the same increment:
   - Item::get_all_contacts() / get_all_contacts(int)  (Java Item.getAllContacts)
   - TileShape::cutout(Polyline) and TileShape::bounding_octagon()
   - DrillItem::get_tree_shape_on_layer(ShapeSearchTree*, int) on the live
     DrillItem of board/Via.h

   Compile traps hit and fixed:
   - board/Connectable.h forward-declares a stray `class TileShape` inside
     namespace freerouting::board, so an unqualified TileShape inside the board
     namespace resolves to that incomplete draft. Fully qualified geometry types
     are required in board/*.cpp.
   - board/Via.h contains a second, stale `class DrillItem : public Item` which
     collides with board/DrillItem.h. Including both in one TU is a redefinition
     error, so the spring-over code must use the Via.h one (Pin and Via derive
     from it; board/DrillItem.h is an orphan that only DrillItem.cpp compiles).
   - geometry::planar::Line has no default constructor, so the substitute-line
     array is collected with push_back instead of being preallocated like the
     Java Line[].

4. Verified Java parity of two suspicious geometry behaviours instead of
   "fixing" them:
   - Polyline::first_corner() is corner(0), i.e. the junction of the lines 0 and 1
     (Java Polyline.corner(0)), not the first point of the polyline.
   - TileShape::entrance_points() and cutout() only scan the lines 1..n-2, so a
     two-corner polyline is never cut and cutout returns it unchanged.
   Both are pinned by tests now. An earlier suspicion that Line::side_of has an
   inverted sign convention turned out to be wrong (Line.sideOf negates twice).

5. Tests: new ShoveTraceAlgo_test.cpp (7), ItemContacts_test.cpp (+6),
   TileShape_test.cpp (+7) -> ctest 673/673 and run_tests PASSED.

Still open for Phase 4.4: ShoveTraceAlgo::check()/insert() with the board
mutation, the RoutingBoard forced-trace insertion + connect_to_trace, then
FoundConnectionInserter itself and the AutorouteEngine::autoroute_connection()
rewiring off the legacy grid A*.

================================================================
Iteration 3, part 2 — WP-10 Increment O (2026-10-02)
  TraceShover.check()/insert() + ShapeTraceEntries cutout/substitute piece
================================================================

1. Ported ShoveTraceAlgo::insert(TileShape, ...) as the full Java
   TraceShover.insert: the empty-shape and outline guards, shove_vias,
   ShapeTraceEntries::store_items, the non-empty shove_via_list failure,
   the substitute_trace_count == 0 early success, the recursion-depth
   guard, contains_trace_tails, cutout_traces, the optional spring-over of
   each piece, the per-segment CalcShapeAndFromSide recursion, the
   changed-area corner loop, the board insertion of the piece, normalize
   with the changed-area clip shape and the !tails_exist_before tail
   cleanup with combine_traces.
2. Ported ShoveTraceAlgo::check(TileShape, ...) and check(LineSegment, ...).
   The instance line-segment overload replaces the old direction
   approximation with Java's test (perpendicular to the shape edge),
   reports the failing obstacle through
   RoutingBoard::get_shove_failing_obstacle(), and keeps the
   numeric_limits<int>::max() "no obstacle" result.
3. Ported ShapeTraceEntries::next_substitute_trace_piece() and made
   cutout_trace()/fast_cutout_trace() mutate the board. The cut trace is
   removed from the item list AND the search tree (RoutingBoard::remove_item
   is list-only), and the Java "pieces[0] == traceLines" identity test is
   replaced by geometric equality.
4. Java-parity findings (all pinned by tests, do not "fix"):
   - LineSegment(const Polyline&, int) needs >= 3 lines; with 2 lines it
     degenerates to (0,0)-(1,0). A test helper was silently producing an
     empty offset shape because of this.
   - IntBox/IntOctagon border_line() return *unit* lines, like Java. Corner
     maths from the infinite lines is right; LineSegment queries on them are
     degenerate. The cut pieces legitimately carry degenerate first/last
     lines.
   - The substitute piece is intentionally NOT on the board during the
     segment recursion (Java only addItem()s after the loop); registering it
     early makes the piece an obstacle of its own recursion.
   - A crossing foreign trace can never be shoved: the foreign net's own cut
     pieces are own-net obstacles of the recursion whose entrance chain
     starts at stack level 0, while calculate_stack_levels() requires the end
     level to be 1, so store_items() fails and insert() returns false with
     the cut piece as the failing obstacle.
   - MoveDrillItemAlgo::shove_vias returns true when it gives up, so insert()
     fails afterwards through the non-empty shove_via_list.
5. Only deliberate divergence: Java's
   SearchTreeManager::reuseEntriesAfterCutout entry recycling is not
   ported; the cut pieces get freshly calculated entries.
6. Tests: ShoveTraceAlgo_test.cpp 7 -> 24 gtests (static and instance
   checks, the insert outcomes, a new ShapeTraceEntriesCutoutTest suite and a
   new ShapeTraceEntriesPieceTest suite) -> ctest 690/690 and run_tests
   PASSED.

Still open for Phase 4.4: the RoutingBoard forced-trace insertion +
connect_to_trace, then FoundConnectionInserter itself and the
AutorouteEngine::autoroute_connection() rewiring off the legacy grid A*.
